   You miss the point. I am not claiming that a transhuman who
recycles humans for extra matter should be embraced. I was supplying
an anecdote to support the claim that most people who attempt
transhumanism in fiction are portrayed as evil. 

   I think the problem is probably just the fact that a transhuman
hero would be boring. Heroes must be outclassed by their enemies
so they can overcome them. But the first transhuman portrayed
as a hero will be outclassed by no one. What will the evil guy
do, threaten him? Threaten a distributed/nanotech robot?
The hero would just sick some nanite disassemblers on him, end of story.

   The same goes for most AI in fiction. AI is usually portrayed
as evil or dangerous. The only sympathetic example I can think of
is the movie "Short Circuit" In Star Trek, for instance, the crew
always attempts to destroy machine intelligence before trying to
understand it. I recall an episode of the old Star Trek where Kirk
finds this colony of androids where the lone human has discovered
a way to upload into android bodies (and they discover at the
end that he wasn't human at all) The guy explains to nurse
Chapel "I will live forever, never get sick, never get old. it's me,
all of my emotions are intact. It's the same person." But she doesn't
believe him and in the end, he gets destroyed.

    Which reminds me, in Lawnmower Man, why was the uploaded
guy so evil? He didn't kill anyone except those who had
abused him when he was ordinary (as typical in Stephen King
stories. The people who "get it", always seem to "deserve it")
However, just because he was so superior, the government and the
scientist were trying to shut him down. 

    Perhaps the more interesting question is why we like fiction
where the hero has to overcome superior obstacles. Why do we
prefer negative news over good news? (or atleast most people do.
The ole "why will people follow a fire truck to watch a fire, but
not a limo to watch a wedding?") Why is distopian fiction so interesting?
My guess is that it is part of my individualist psyche. I like seeing
people *overcome* things, overcome impossible odds, overcome
unstoppable forces, etc.


-Ray
 

