Date: Fri, 16 Sep 1994 23:38:01 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-198] superheroes

Scattered remarks on superheroes.

One of my favorite superheroes is Daredevil.  He (Matt Murdock) 
has supremely acute senses, but had to learn to use them; and he
trains very hard to keep strong and agile.  He has a strong sense
of poetic justice.

Two new titles combine the superhero formula with titillation more 
successfully than most: The Exhibitionist, who has super strength 
when she's aroused, and Doctor Dare, who gets temporary powers 
with every orgasm.  ("If only those savages had ravished me, 
we wouldn't be tied to this log!")

Henry Pym (Giantman / Antman / Yellowjacket) and his ex-wife 
Janet van Dyne (the Wasp) are biologists who transformed
themselves.  Hank McCoy (the Beast) was originally a somewhat 
odd-looking human with abnormal agility and strength.  In the 
middle Seventies or so, he experimented on himself (I forget 
what his intent was) and grew fur and fangs.

I heard it mentioned long ago that Reed Richards (Mister Fantastic)
- or maybe it was Nick Fury - was given long life by some alien
Power; and that his brother(?), now old, was driven mad with envy.
Lately it is written that the super-soldier serum that created
Captain America, though it extended his youth, is now poisoning
him.  The Flash (whose power came from an accident in his lab) 
burned himself out in "Crisis".  Adam Warlock killed his future
self rather than allow him/self to become The Magus, an evil god.

Vance Astro, leader of the Guardians of the Galaxy, hibernated for
centuries on a sublight spaceship.  If his copper skinsuit is
damaged, he says, he'll crumble to dust.  (But lately, I believe,
he has been relieved of that fear.)

My reading of the Dark Phoenix story is not quite the same as
Tim's.  It's not simply that power corrupts.  Phoenix was good
_until_ Mastermind/Wyngarde messed with her mind - and came to wish
he hadn't.  (His punishment wasn't oneness with the universe, was
it? I thought it was more like the "Infinite Perspective Vortex"
that someone tried to inflict on Zaphod: he looked upon the face 
of god and overloaded.)  Lead us not into temptation.  A few years
later, someone turned Storm - a "total" claustrophobe - into a
statue; she snapped and caused hurricanes.

Godhood isn't always bad.  The Kree captain Mar-Vell is "cosmically
aware" and it doesn't seem to hurt him any.  Warlock, after his
encounter with the Magus, had a "multi-compartmental" mind which
sometimes came in handy.  Starhawk(?), of the Guardians of the
Galaxy, kept saying "accept the word of One who Knows", but I
didn't read long enough to understand what his schtick was.

And then there's THOR #337.  Beta Ray Bill was chosen to be
champion of his people, to guard their rear as they fled from a
star-hopping demon horde.  He was transformed (into something
resembling a broad-shouldered rat).  He ran into Thor, who dropped 
his hammer and after a minute reverted to Don Blake.  Bill said 
"Where did the big dude go?", whacked Blake's walking-stick in 
annoyance - and was transformed, for "whoever lifts this hammer, 
if he be worthy, shall wield the power of Thor."  Odin was 
momentarily fooled ...

Have there been any stupid superheroes, besides the Hulk?  (The
obvious answer will be ignored.)  On the other hand, how many have
enhanced their brainpower?  Ozymandias (Adrian Veidt) of the
Watchmen is "the smartest man in the world" but we're told nothing
of how he got that way.  (P.R.?)

Asterix got strength from a druidic potion, and Goofy became Super
Goof when he ate super-goobers; are there any other pill-popping
superguys or has the War On Different Drugs driven them underground?

Where does the Sandman's mind reside when he's oozing out of jail?

I hope you'll pardon my going on at such length.

