From: aberenzw@minerva.cis.yale.edu (Adam)
Date: Sat, 17 Sep 1994 14:56:11 +0500
Subject: [#94-9-206] what's next?

I apologize for the apparent irrelevance/irreverence in this question; this
is my first post to this (or, if you must know the truth, any)
newsgroup/zine-type-of-thing.    I've been following the extropians list
for several weeks now, and I'm bothered by something.

I am encountered by brilliance here on the shores of the net, the web...
clear heralds of drastic change, assured boasters of knowledge.

I shivered when I read the first postings to this list.

Here's my bug: I'm hearing a lot of talk about the future.   Is that not
what extropians play with, develop, and guide?
I hear talk about nanocyborgs racing though my veins, searching for
intruding cells, viruses, offending chemicals, and then injecting medicines
constructed molecule by molecule, atom by atom, to kill, destroy, or
decompose them.
I read that uploading my brain to a computer will probably be the first
major extropian change to rebuild society, (bodies cast aside, perhaps).
But human brain uploading _must_ be preceded by certain other events in the
history of science, which is about to become the history of mankind.
        before we get storage sizes up to the needs to simulate a human
brain, don't we need to pass the stage where we can store immense personal
computers _at_least_ in something that will fit on our wrists! (let me know
if you're interested, I have some practical ideas for this).      in other
words, in order to get to such a high degree of complexity, I expect to see
trials, at least a progress of increasing complexity, referenced from what
we can see today.   Is the problem just that there are technologies that
exist, but that I'm not seeing?
        I know we live in a market economy, which means that I'm sure I
never see the highest end of technology; its just too expensive. but a
market economy means that if there really _were_ existing technologies to
be exploited, especially in this job-hungry environment, it seems to me
that they would be pounced on! seized immediately by anyone with brains,
implemented into real-life useability, and sold!

So where are we?  Computers are getting faster, granted, and more powerful,
and pleasurably, smaller.  But in terms of miniaturization, how can we get
nanocyborgs without pocket robots first? at least for the blind! c'mon!!  I
assume all of you are in the workforce, if not powering it.    So when are
the government and the economy going to log on and fund research, allow
more growth, or just give up and die?  When is the populace going to yearn
for head implants and nuclear plants and artificially living plants?  When
are they going to get bored with bosnia and OJ and crack cocaine?  I say,
not till they, too,  can _at_least_ see in the dark, and have music beamed
to their eardrums, and have virtual sex that blows anything mundanely
mechanical off the planet!

What I'm trying to self-deny is the fear that this new society will not
ever be; the horror of its nonexistence disturbs me.  If extropians have
real power to put these things into our world, then _real_ acceleration
will begin.  With the force of the entire educated world behind it, I would
doubt no such predictions of space exploration, eradication of DNA and
molecular life altogether....
(what if somebody was left, some meat freak who wouldn't go over? would he
unplug us all? what if there was enough for a society, a gene pool? if they
collectively decided to unplug us? whose genes would survive then? is there
a certain balance between the two human societies, a mathematical
equilibrium that we could monitor,  that would arise?)

So am I wrong?  Am I missing current technologies that would blow me away,
if I only had two and a half million to spend?  In my opinion, that's not
good enough- it's got to be accessible enough to attract the force of the
mass populace, if one expects to see the kind of technological explosion
that is being predicted, even counted upon.

I'm still undergraduate- not yet in the workforce.  I don't have the
knowledge or experience yet to bring something truly extropian into the
world.  For that's what we must do.  Isn't it our responsibility as
scientists and extropians to do it ourselves, to create the future that we
plan so intricately?

It would excite me more to hear talk about what you are going to create
tomorrow, than what _I_ will create twenty years from now.  If you are
correct in predicting the spread of extropian reality, then by the time
it's my turn to power the workforce, certain first steps must have been
already taken.
Please reassure my doubtful mind.

AB
